{:now [{:claim "The law of reflection is a linear map"
        :modern "A ray with direction d hitting a surface with unit normal n leaves along d - 2(d·n)n, the Householder reflection; it keeps lengths and the angle with the normal."
        :tex "d' = d - 2\\,(d\\cdot n)\\,n"}
       {:claim "Alhazen's problem is a quartic"
        :modern "The reflection point on a circle from two given points satisfies a polynomial equation of degree four in the coordinates of the point; it has no ruler-and-compass solution in general, which is why Ibn al-Haytham needed conic sections and why it is solved today by a root finder."
        :tex "\\frac{P\\times(A-P)}{|A-P|} + \\frac{P\\times(B-P)}{|B-P|} = 0"}
       {:claim "The focus of a parabola"
        :modern "Every ray parallel to the axis of y² = 4fx is reflected through (f, 0): the property behind satellite dishes, reflecting telescopes and solar concentrators."
        :tex "y^2 = 4fx \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{every axial ray passes through } (f, 0)"}]
 :fields [{:field :mechanics-physics :label "Optics" :how "Ray tracing in geometric optics and computer graphics computes reflection points exactly as in Alhazen's problem." :example "Specular highlights on a sphere in a renderer."}
          {:field :geometry :how "The reflection point is an intersection of a circle with a hyperbola, a problem of conics in the tradition of Apollonius." :example "Huygens' and Barrow's solutions of the same problem in the seventeenth century."}
          {:field :analysis :how "The volume of the paraboloid by sums of powers of the integers is an integral computed before the calculus (Suter 1912; text not yet in hand)." :example "Archimedes' sums for the conoids, carried one power further."}]
